            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "this was very easy to make. the only change i would make to the recipe is to add more water. to reduce the mangos to a sauce i needed a lot more water, but after i did that, it was great! my guests put this over white cake and loved it."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Foody"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was a pleasant fruit sauce that wasn't very sweet to go with my sweeter cakes. I used the yellow skinned mangoes which isn't as stong flavoured/fragrant as the red/green skinned ones. So I adjusted the tartness to my taste and added a pinch of salt to bring out the flavour. I did use a little cornstarch and water mixture to thicken the sauce more.",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-17T06:24:29.387Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "labell57"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe was so easy! One thing I did to make sure it was nice and creamy was to put it in my blender on low for just a few seconds. It puréed it nicely. I also added a bit more brown sugar as it was pretty tart and I’m using as a dessert topping for guests tonight. I can’t for the life of me figure out why to lady who said she needed to add more water to reduce the mangoes had to do so. Mangoes have a good deal of natural juice, so adding more water would have made it soupy. I mashed the chunks of mangoes on the bottom of the sauce pan to help smooth it out while it was cooking. A good thing to do. I followed the ingredients exactly except for the brown sugar, and it turned out perfectly.",
            "datePublished": "2018-10-05T20:58:19.170Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Beth"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Quick and easy and tased good on pancakes. I used frozen mango which worked fine but was more difficult to mash, will cook longer next time. I put in pan with water and cooked on low until thawed then added other ingredients. May add more water next time as there wasn't much liquid sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2018-08-19T20:23:09.867Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "JERSEYGIRL1"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I had 5 mangos that needed to be used right away. I wanted a sauce of some kind that I could use for chicken or pork, and sweet dishes. I made this recipe as written, and it's exactly what I was looking for. It makes a delicious sauce that is easy to adjust the flavor and use for savory and sweet, or eat as it is. It's also a perfect recipe to make and freeze. Next time I think I'll leave out the brown sugar since mangos are already pretty sweet.",
            "datePublished": "2025-08-04T18:18:55.359Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Betty P"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Tasty! I used twice as much brown sugar, twice as much OJ and probably an extra T of water. It was quite thick without that. Used it on Seth's pancakes to make them soft enough after surgery.",
            "datePublished": "2014-02-16T10:26:54.517Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "WeirdAuntMartha"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was a great way to quickly use up the mangoes that were getting away from me. A dollop of this, some fresh mango, fresh whipped cream, and angel food cake made for a great dessert!",
            "datePublished": "2015-07-11T02:21:16.493Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MUNECASTACE"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did not use the exact measurements, I just used one mango and adjusted it to my taste. I used a lot more orange juice and water than the recipe called for. I also found it helpful to mash the mango with a fork while sauteeing it in the pan. I put this on top of a betty crocker vanilla cake, it was DELICIOUS.",
            "datePublished": "2008-06-20T08:54:12.937Z"
          },
          {
